WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.100
Quarto Dashboards is a new
feature that is coming to

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.945
Quarto in the 1.4 release.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:13.640
And it's something that we
really decided to add on the

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.650
heels of the most
recent Posit conference.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.800
And so it wasn't originally
on the plan for 1.4,

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.930
but we decided to take a little
bit of extra time and see if we

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:21.920
could squeeze it in.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:28.100
Obviously, the goal of
Quarto Dashboards is pretty simple.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.900
Make it easy
to build compelling interactive

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.740
dashboards using R,
Python, Julia, Observable.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.450
And very much

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:40.130
sort of heritage of
flexdashboard. So very much

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.905
bring flexdashboard in that
type of experience into Quarto.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.410
Incorporate everything
we've learned,

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.300
everything the
company has built,

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.675
and try to bring that together into
a really great experience for our users.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.430
Dashboards in Quarto are
pretty straightforward.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.130
They're basically
composed of cards.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.680
So you can think about cards
as just sort of the units--

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.860
the atomic units that you're
arranging in a dashboard.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.560
And you arrange those
cards into rows and columns

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.580
to lay them out. And then you
can use things like pages,

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:14.120
tabsets, and sidebars to
incorporate more advanced layout,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.600
to incorporate controls and
other things that might need to

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.950
appear in the dashboard.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.770
But with those simple
building blocks,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.830
it's actually pretty easy
to build some cool dashboards.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.640
So let's build a simple
dashboard. Let's see if we can

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.595
do this. So let's just
start from scratch.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.070
I've got a QMD that
I've got going here.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.205
And I'm just going to start
with the front matter.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.080
My dashboard.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.710
And let's make it
format dashboard.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.575
And then let's just put in

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.025
for now, we're going
to explore my lack of

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.295
our programming skills.
But let's just put in

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.410
a simple static plot to start.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.225
And then let's have a
look at what we get.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.100
And so--

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.560
oops. We're not quite fitting
on the screen. Hang on.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:06.780
Oh. There we go.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.289
And so you can see
now I've gotten--

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:13.320
OK. I guess I've gotten a
little title bar, I've gotten

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.415
one big card.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.210
And so you can see the
treatment is already a little

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.600
different than just a simple
web page because we're filling

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.820
the whole screen. And in fact,
as we add elements to this

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.420
dashboard, like let's add a
second plot to this dashboard,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.160
it knows enough to lay
things out appropriately.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.190
So it just made
to two different cards here

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.580
and the cards contain
the visualization.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:39.630
But of course,

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:44.270
I may want to start to do some cooler
things to tune this up a little bit. So

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.713
let's start by putting a
title on some of these cards.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.245
So let's go title

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.450
cards plot.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.900
And then we'll add
a title down here.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.120
Empty cards

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:58.560
plot.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:01.580
And we're

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.020
starting to see, OK.
Now, we're getting--

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:04.755
it's getting better.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.400
I wonder if those might
look better side by side.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.565
Well, in Quarto Dashboards, you use
headings to arrange things. And so

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.950
by adding a simple heading, I
can basically say, OK. I'd like to--

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.865
I guess maybe the way to explain
it is to do this. Is to say,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.160
dashboards have sort
of an orientation,

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.290
which by default, is rows.
So by default it's saying,

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.330
just everything that
shows up is a row.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.480
I could change that
and say, actually like,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.100
let's make everything
columns instead.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.830
And so when I do that,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.930
we'll actually get the
layout now into columns.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.110
Similarly, if I said, let's go
back to rows, just a good old

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.655
basic orientation that we had.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.410
If I said, I want to make
a row explicitly, I can add a heading

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.015
and say, here's a
row that I made.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:02.370
And so I made a row and the
row has two things side by side in it.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.060
It'll get even easier to see
if I do something make another row

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.505
and put some content
in it. Here is

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:11.595
some content.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.719
So now I have made a dashboard

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.790
that basically has here,

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.970
a row with some content in it.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.840
And then here a row
with the plots in it.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.900
And you can see that looks
very straightforward.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:35.930
And so that gives you the
basic idea of arranging things. Now,

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.135
you can keep going.
So I could say,

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.690
I'm going to make a
column inside here.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.200
And then I'm going to
make another column,

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.995
and I will put another
plot inside that column.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:51.620
So

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.425
I'll put another
slot cars in here.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:58.550
And so you can see,

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.420
you can make some pretty
fancy layouts with some pretty simple

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:06.510
constructs with just using
headings to arrange content.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:08.380
But

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:09.740
maybe

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.600
you want to do something

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:13.150
cooler.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.430
So for example, maybe

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.420
instead of putting these
side by side like that,

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:21.575
we want to make them in tabs.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:27.370
So we can just add a tab
set to our column heading right there.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.340
And now we've got them in tabs.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:32.305
So that's pretty cool.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.090
But maybe we don't
even want them in tabs.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:38.815
Maybe we want them
on separate pages.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.390
So why don't we put a heading
one right here and we'll make a page

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:45.900
and a page,

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:48.110
page 2.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.040
And now we've arranged them on
completely standalone pages. And in fact,

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.625
we could have very fancy layouts
on each of these two pages.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.310
And so you can see that

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.220
some important
concepts at work here.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.500
So one important concept is these
code cells are very, very primary.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:11.530
So these code cells are
automatically making cards.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.270
And so that means you don't
really have to do a lot of

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:16.420
work. You basically can
make code cells that create

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:20.140
visualizations and then place
some simple markdown around the

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.400
outside to provide the
layout and orientation.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.635
The second, as you can see,

00:06:26.635 --> 00:06:31.330
the headings and the markdown that
you need to add to create

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:34.300
these layouts is really
straightforward and hopefully

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:35.710
very, very intuitive.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.820
So that gives you some of
the basic ideas. Now, of course,

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:41.785
there's more you can do.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:47.365
You can do things like add
additional navigation. So I could say like

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.110
I want a Twitter sharing link

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.470
and then I want a
GitHub link to the repo.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:56.890
And then we'll automatically

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:02.230
do the right thing and figure out your
GitHub URL and put a share in URL.

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:05.200
So you can very quickly get
some pretty nice things.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.700
There are more things you can
do than just cards. So for example,

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:18.095
you could do a row that's
got what are called value boxes.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:22.920
Value boxes, you probably
are familiar with from bslib.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.285
But if I want to make
a value box here,

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:31.090
I can make a code cell
and then I can just say,

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:32.905
this code cell is a value box.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.450
And then this code cell really just
needs to return a list that describes

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.250
what we want to show
here. So let's just say

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:45.270
my

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.130
value is 26.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:51.795
The color is going to be red.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.620
And let's even
put an icon on it

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.020
for like envelope.

00:08:00.305 --> 00:08:03.245
And then let's make two of those
just because it'll look a little cooler.

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:06.885
So now we've got

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.110
a red and a green value
box that we've created.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:15.120
And there they are. They've
showed up just as you'd expect.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.520
And what's great about this
is now these value boxes,

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.380
of course, since they're
built programmatically,

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:20.500
can be responsive.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:23.145
So obviously, they
can be red and green.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.015
The icons can update to
reflect thing or reflect the status.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:30.630
And so it's super
straightforward now to make

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:34.830
these types of arrangements
using this Quarto Dashboard.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:35.840
Now,

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.780
I think the other thing to
think about and one other area

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:43.034
that we focused on was trying
to create experiences that were great

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:45.915
inside notebooks as well.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:49.020
And so here I've got
a Jupyter Notebook.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.240
I was showing you a
Quarto markdown document.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.675
Now I've got a Jupyter Notebook
that's built in a dashboard as well.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.400
And part of what's great about
using the cells as sort of

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.450
atomic units,

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.900
as you can see, this notebook,

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.055
which basically makes
a pretty nice dashboard

00:09:08.245 --> 00:09:09.940
has some markdown cells,

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:13.360
but really is just a set of
code cells with a little bit of

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.910
markdown around it to arrange
it. And if we actually

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:21.430
open up a terminal here
and actually preview this.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:24.290
Quarto

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:27.970
review Gapminder.

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:32.420
So if we actually preview
this Jupyter Notebook,

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:36.730
we will--

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:37.230
oops.

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:41.055
We will see, here's the
dashboard that that creates. And so

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:46.650
you can go and
create pretty great authoring experiences

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:50.655
inside notebooks even for building
these really nice and robust

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:53.670
auto dashboards.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:56.150
And so

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:02.450
I think the last little bit
for me to talk about is

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:07.050
just give you a quick
look, a preview of some interactivity.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.160
So let me just get back to--

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.510
so a couple examples

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.350
of interactive dashboards.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:18.540
And so obviously,

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.750
the static dashboards that I showed
you help you understand the layout.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:24.480
But here's a nice dashboard

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:30.080
that shows some of
the interactivity and how straightforward.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.010
It is to incorporate things
like shiny into these dashboards.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:33.510
So

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:35.840
this is all pretty cool.

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:38.410
But what's really cool is when
you look at the actual source

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:42.640
behind it and you see how
straightforward the source is.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:43.640
We've made a sidebar,

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:48.770
we have the main plot, we
have the data, the data page.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.580
And then we've just mixed some
code cells in there that create

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:53.540
the various inputs

00:10:54.755 --> 00:10:56.330
that actually render the plot.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:59.210
And all of that works
just beautifully.

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:01.700
And so it's really
straightforward to make both

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:06.430
fancy layouts and also
pretty impressive interactive dashboards.

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.940
As I said, this is
coming in Quarto 1.4.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.865
I think we anticipate
that early next year.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:15.040
And

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:18.300
I wanted to highlight, I
think, one other thing,

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.240
which is over the
past two weeks,

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.180
we've been working
hard on this feature.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:25.850
But the rest of the company
has chipped in a great way by

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:27.605
helping us both test

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.180
and generate a huge number
of sample dashboards.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:35.270
We appreciate everyone's
effort and support doing that.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:39.170
I have found a number of really
great bugs as a result of all

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:40.970
that testing. A few of
which are still open,

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:41.975
but I'm getting through them.

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:46.500
And we also have probably 20
great samples on the Quarto website,

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:50.210
most of which were contributed by
other members of the company. So

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.520
thanks to all for
your support as we

00:11:54.945 --> 00:11:57.360
grind really hard to try
to get this into Quarto 1.4

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:59.430
unexpectedly. So thanks all and

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:01.530
hopefully people
like what they see. [MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:02.430
PLAYING]

